exemplar.en:
Decisions. The shipped index.html and form.js in source-code carry four linked defects. form.reset() empties both inputs after an invalid submit. document.activeElement.blur() sends focus to the document body. No field carries aria-describedby and index.html has no element with id error-summary, so no summary can receive programmatic focus. The email check value.includes('@') fails the brief rule of exactly one at sign plus at least one full stop after that at sign, and because form.js performs its DOM lookups at load time, Run 3 of source-handler-test throws before isValidEmail can run. The rule requires no character before the at sign, so '@example.com' is valid while 'relay.example@' and 'a@b@c.com' are not. In Run 1 the empty submit produces both error messages. In Run 2 the trimmed name 'Relay' passes, so only 'Contact email is invalid.' appears, and form.reset() then clears both fields while focus lands on the document body.

Corrected markup and handler:
```html
<form id="request-form" novalidate>
  <label for="displayName">Display name</label>
  <input id="displayName" name="displayName" aria-describedby="displayName-error">
  <p id="displayName-error"></p>
  <label for="contactEmail">Contact email</label>
  <input id="contactEmail" name="contactEmail" aria-describedby="contactEmail-error">
  <p id="contactEmail-error"></p>
  <button type="submit">Submit</button>
</form>
<div id="error-summary" tabindex="-1"></div>
<p id="form-status"></p>
```
```js
function isValidEmail(value) {
  const atIndex = value.indexOf('@')
  if (atIndex === -1) {
    return false
  }
  if (value.indexOf('@', atIndex + 1) !== -1) {
    return false
  }
  return value.slice(atIndex + 1).includes('.')
}

function initForm() {
  const form = document.getElementById('request-form')
  const displayName = document.getElementById('displayName')
  const contactEmail = document.getElementById('contactEmail')
  const nameError = document.getElementById('displayName-error')
  const emailError = document.getElementById('contactEmail-error')
  const summary = document.getElementById('error-summary')
  const status = document.getElementById('form-status')

  form.addEventListener('submit', function (event) {
    event.preventDefault()
    nameError.textContent = ''
    emailError.textContent = ''
    summary.textContent = ''
    status.textContent = ''

    const messages = []
    if (displayName.value.trim() === '') {
      nameError.textContent = 'Display name is required.'
      messages.push('Display name is required.')
    }
    if (!isValidEmail(contactEmail.value)) {
      emailError.textContent = 'Contact email is invalid.'
      messages.push('Contact email is invalid.')
    }

    if (messages.length > 0) {
      messages.forEach(function (message) {
        const line = document.createElement('p')
        line.textContent = message
        summary.appendChild(line)
      })
      summary.focus()
      return
    }

    status.textContent = 'Thanks, your request was recorded.'
  })
}

if (typeof document !== 'undefined') {
  initForm()
}
```
Checks. Re-run runs 1 and 2 on the corrected build. An empty submit must keep both fields empty in value while focus moves to #error-summary, and Tab must still run display name, contact email, submit with no stop on the summary. Submitting 'Relay' with 'relay.example' must keep both typed strings and keep focus on #error-summary. A non-empty trimmed name with 'relay@example.com' must show the confirmation in #form-status with no error lines. Calling isValidEmail directly must return false for 'relay.example@' and 'a@b@c.com' and true for '@example.com'. Loading the corrected file without a document must not throw. I did not verify real screen reader output, so that stays an open item for Priya Raman to schedule.

